    notes: "CO added - verified this session (statute walk + full-library audit; content itself EDITED this session WY added 2026-08-21 (full-library cross-check pass): generic mechanics, no CO-specific statutory language or number, confirmed no conflict with WY findings from this project. | NE extended (session 6): the non-waiver sentence isn't prohibited in Nebraska the way the exculpation clauses were in Kansas — it's just insufficient on its own to satisfy §76-1433's after-the-breach requirement. No override needed; see edu-late-rent-reservation-fix-ne for the landlord-facing explanation of why lease text alone doesn't solve this and what actually does. | MN extended (Minnesota session, whole-library audit): generic non-waiver mechanics compatible with Minn. Stat. § 504B.177(a) — statute requires the written agreement to specify when the fee is imposed, which the {{late_fee_grace_days}} mechanic satisfies. Paired with late-fee-limit-mn for the 8% cap. | NE REMOVED 2026-08-31 (NE re-audit) — superseded by late-fee-ne. §76-1433 read verbatim from nebraskalegislature.gov. This clause's phrase 'or to pursue any other remedy available under this Lease' is UNQUALIFIED and therefore purports to preserve termination for the very breach §76-1433 says is waived. §5a.1 JUDGMENT — STATE-DRIVEN, not uniform: the fix was deliberately NOT propagated to CO, WY, or MN. A non-waiver clause preserving termination after accepting late rent is genuinely valuable where contract can preserve it, and none of those three has been shown to carry Nebraska's express statutory timing rule. Narrowing all four states would surrender real protection in three of them to solve a problem only one has. Structurally consistent with Kansas, which also took its own late-fee-ks override. | ND ADDED 2026-09-06 (ND re-audit, exhaustive generic-clause audit prompted by the checklist-to-CSV reconciliation screen). ND's ORIGINAL whole-library audit was written up in its log but largely never executed against the CSV -- ND was shipped 'complete' with 18 displayable LEASE_CLAUSE rows against 61-75 for CO/WY/KS/NE/MN. This row is generic boilerplate carrying no ND-specific statutory interaction; ND's ch. 47-16 was read in full from primary text this session and no conflict was identified. LIABILITY-LANGUAGE CHECK: North Dakota has NO enumerated prohibited-lease-provisions statute (confirmed this session -- § 47-16-02 is a maximum-term cap, and § 47-16-13.3 supplies only case-by-case unconscionability). The exculpation exposure that forced fixes to this clause family in KS and NE therefore does not arise for ND, same reasoning as the WY addendum. 5a.1 JUDGMENT: UNIFORM -- states field changed, clause TEXT unchanged, no re-review owed on existing tags. ND has NO statutory late-fee cap (confirmed: full read of ch. 47-16 contains no late-fee provision), so no figure conflict arises. Same non-outcome as WY. | SD ADDED 2026-09-07 (SD re-audit, Tier A of the L.2 exhaustive generic-clause audit). SD displayed only 13 LEASE_CLAUSE rows against 60-75 for every other state; 46 generics had no SD tag and no SD override. Triaged per L.2, not bulk-extended. Tier A = boilerplate with no SD statutory interaction, or self-limiting text that defers to applicable law. S5a.1 JUDGMENT: adding a state tag changes `states` only - no bodyText, rule_type or content_type change - so no other tagged state's rendering is affected and NO propagation to other state logs is owed. State-driven, not uniform. SD sets no statutory late-fee cap and no mandatory grace period, so the landlord-set fields create no conflict. The non-waiver sentence is also safe: SD has no waiver-by-acceptance rule of the NE S76-1433 / KS type. MATERIAL GAP CLOSED: SD previously had NO late-fee clause of any kind, and because SD law leaves late fees entirely to the lease, an SD lease generated without this clause could not charge a late fee at all. | FL: Applies as written (tag-first screen, instruction 26). No late-fee cap or grace-period statute in Fla. Stat. ch. 83 Part II (full read, §§83.40-83.684); boundary: Part II only, other titles not searched. Local fee caps are preempted by Fla. Stat. §83.425 ('fees charged by the landlord'; s. 1, ch. 2023-314). The amount stays subject to §83.45 (unconscionability). The clause does not designate the fee as rent; under §83.43(12) only payments 'designated as rent in a written rental agreement' are rent. Library default (Taylor, NJ 2026-09-26): fees are not labeled rent. s5a.1: states-only change, no propagation owed. | FL: FL research pass 2026-09-26 (advanced research, once; report in the conversation). Trial-level Florida decisions (secondary) treat a 3-day notice that demands a late charge not designated as rent as defective; the library's no-'additional rent' posture avoids that. No statewide late-fee cap found (not a full-text verified absence). | ND 2026-09-28: the LIABILITY-LANGUAGE CHECK above is withdrawn as to any clause disclaiming liability for the landlord's own violation of law. North Dakota does have enumerated bans outside its landlord-tenant title: N.D.C.C. §§9-08-02 (exculpation), 9-08-03 (penalties), 9-08-04 (liquidated damages) and 9-08-05 (restricting enforcement). See edu-prohibited-lease-terms-nd. | ND (added 2026-09-28): the absence of a cap is confirmed statewide, not only in ch. 47-16 (edu-no-late-fee-cap-nd). North Dakota's operative limit is not a cap: §9-08-03 voids contractual penalties outright and §9-08-04 voids advance-fixed damages unless actual damage would be impracticable or extremely difficult to fix. So a late fee must be defensible as an estimate of actual loss. Three cap bills have failed (2021 HB 1440, HB 1442; 2025 SB 2236). | GA: Applies as written. No Georgia late-fee cap or grace period: confirmed absent statute-wide on the official O.C.G.A. full-text search 2026-09-28 (`edu-no-fee-caps-ga`). Late fees may be demanded in the 3-business-day notice to vacate or pay (O.C.G.A. § 44-7-50(c)) and retained from the deposit (O.C.G.A. § 44-7-34(a)). The non-waiver sentence conflicts with no Georgia statute; case law on waiver by accepting late rent exists and is NOT relied on (instruction 16 flag). s5a.1: states-only change, no propagation owed. | SC: Tagged. No statutory late-fee cap or grace period (confirmed absent, `edu-no-late-fee-cap-sc`); late charges are part of 'rent' (S.C. Code Ann. § 27-40-210(11)). Penalty doctrine is case law, not read. | PA: Tagged. No late-fee statute (`edu-no-late-fee-cap-pa`, confirmed absent). No statutory acceptance-waiver rule like Nebraska's and Alabama's (see `late-fee-ne`); whether accepting rent after a notice to quit waives it is case law, not read, so the base's non-waiver sentence stays. Penalty doctrine: case law. s5a.1: states-only change, no propagation owed.",
