    topicKey: "repair-remedies-notice",
    notes: "TX: DORMANT ROW REWRITTEN AND ACTIVATED 2026-09-25 (instruction 21); retitled from 'Repair Timeline'. Tex. Prop. Code ch. 92 read in full, section-open, 2026-09-25 (Justia '2025 Texas Statutes' host copy supplied by Taylor; per-section history lines checked - latest amendments 89th Leg., R.S. (2025); CONFIRMED 2026-09-25 against the official statutes.capitol.texas.gov ch. 92 page supplied by Taylor: no 1st or 2nd called-session amendment; text matches section for section - TX log s14). Controlling text: s92.056 (history ends Acts 2015, 84th Leg., R.S., Ch. 1198 (S.B. 1367), eff. Jan. 1, 2016) and s92.0561 (Amended by Acts 2025, 89th Leg., R.S., Ch. 732 (H.B. 2037), Sec. 1, eff. Sept. 1, 2025 - the enacted change, per the Senate Research Center analysis (research pass 2026-09-25): H.B. 2037 replaced the former requirement of a repairer 'listed in the yellow or business pages ... or in the classified advertising section of a newspaper' with 'an independent company, contractor, or repairman', licensed where the municipality requires. The clause text matches the current law). What the pre-rigor row got right: the 7-day rebuttable presumption exists (s92.056(d)). What it got wrong or missed: (1) it framed a 7-day repair as 'presumed reasonable' with no link to the notice machinery the presumption operates in (s92.056(b)(1),(3)-(4); receipt rule s92.056(c)); (2) it omitted the actual lease requirement - s92.056(g): 'A lease must contain language in underlined or bold print that informs the tenant of the remedies available under this section and Section 92.0561.' The row is therefore now the REQUIRED s92.056(g) statement, carrying the presumption accurately. LAYOUT REQUIREMENT - needs a bold/underline field (TX log s5). Condition 'tenant not delinquent' appears in s92.052(a)(2) and s92.056(b)(6). Deduction cap s92.0561(b)-(c); contractor rules (f); bill and receipt (j). Remedy triggers for repair-and-deduct (sewage/flooding immediate; water cessation and heat/cooling 3 days; other official-certified conditions 7 days) are in edu-repair-duty-tx rather than lease text. | Prior (pre-rigor) body: A repair completed within 7 days after Landlord receives notice from Tenant is presumed reasonable under Texas law. Landlord will make a diligent effort to repair conditions it is responsible for under this Lease within that time, recognizing that a longer period may be reasonable depending on the nature of the repair and the availability of parts or labor.",
