    notes: "CO added - verified this session (statute walk + full-library audit; content itself unchanged WY added 2026-08-21 (full-library cross-check pass): generic mechanics, no CO-specific statutory language or number, confirmed no conflict with WY findings from this project. | NE added (session 1): Neb. Rev. Stat. §76-1423(3) requires a fixed 24-hour written notice (not a 'reasonable notice' standard like WY/KS) — matches the clause's built-in 24-hour figure directly, extended per Taylor's direction (Flag 3). | Display-collision bug fixed (session 7, full-library supersedes audit): CO removed — already covered by a state-specific override that supersedes this row; both versions were displaying simultaneously before this fix. | OH: R.C. 5321.04(A)(8): reasonable notice required, 24 hours presumed reasonable absent contrary evidence; entry only at reasonable times. Remedies at (B): actual damages, injunction, statutory attorney's fees, or termination. R.C. 5321.05(B): tenant shall not unreasonably withhold consent. The clause's built-in 24-hour figure matches Ohio's presumed-reasonable standard directly, the same reason NE's fixed 24-hour rule let this clause extend to NE without an override. | NV: Read in full 2026-09-24 against NRS 118A (revisor text Rev. 4/15/2026, 2025 session incorporated). Applies as written: 118A.330(3) requires at least 24 hours' notice and entry at reasonable times during normal business hours absent emergency or the tenant's express consent for that entry. 118A.330(4) - landlord has NO other right of access (court order, abandonment/surrender, 118A.440 excepted). | TX: Read in full 2026-09-25 against Tex. Prop. Code ch. 92 (full chapter, section-open; Justia 2025 host copy with per-section history lines). Applies as written. No statutory entry-notice rule in ch. 92 (full read) - see edu-no-entry-notice-statute-tx; clause is a contractual term. Other codes not searched. | NJ: added 2026-09-26 (tag-first screen, instruction 26). Applies as written. For multiple dwellings the occupant must give access on reasonable notification, ordinarily one day, and immediately in safety or structural emergencies (N.J.A.C. 5:10-5.1(c)); the clause's 24 hours matches. No statute read sets an entry rule for 1-2 unit rentals. s5a.1: states-only change, no propagation owed. | GA: Applies as written. Georgia has no statutory entry-notice rule: confirmed absent statute-wide (official full-text search 2026-09-28; `edu-no-entry-notice-statute-ga`). The 24-hour notice is contractual. s5a.1: states-only change, no propagation owed. | NC: Applies as written. North Carolina has no statute on landlord entry, notice or hours (confirmed absent, full-text search 2026-09-28; edu-no-entry-notice-statute-nc), so the contractual 24 hours governs. s5a.1: states-only change, no propagation owed. | SC: Tagged. 24 hours' notice and reasonable times (S.C. Code Ann. § 27-40-530(c)); emergency entry without consent (S.C. Code Ann. § 27-40-530(b)(1)). The clause does not claim the no-notice periodic-services entry, which exists only if conspicuously written into the lease (S.C. Code Ann. § 27-40-530(b)(2)): opt-in `periodic-services-entry-sc`. Remedies: a tenant who refuses lawful access can be enjoined or the lease terminated, with damages and fees; a landlord's unlawful or harassing entry gives the tenant the same (S.C. Code Ann. § 27-40-780). | PA: Tagged. No statutory entry notice (`edu-no-entry-notice-rule-pa`, confirmed absent); 24 hours by contract, as in the Pennsylvania REALTORS® Form RL (rev. 9/17, rel. 1/18) ¶11. s5a.1: states-only change, no propagation owed.",
