  "pet-policy": {
    ruleTypes: ["RECOMMENDED"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-08-21",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-28",
    topicKey: "pet-policy",
    notes: "CO added - verified this session (statute walk + full-library audit; content itself unchanged WY added 2026-08-21 (full-library cross-check pass): generic mechanics, no CO-specific statutory language or number, confirmed no conflict with WY findings from this project. | NE extended (session 2, whole-library generic-clause audit): generic mechanics, no NE-specific statutory language or number, confirmed no conflict with NE findings from this project. | States KS,NE removed (session 5, full liability-audit fix): superseded by state-specific override(s) addressing K.S.A. 58-2547(a)(4)/(a)(1) and/or Neb. Rev. Stat. §76-1415(1)(d) exposure found in this session's full-library liability audit. | ND ADDED 2026-09-06 (ND re-audit, exhaustive generic-clause audit prompted by the checklist-to-CSV reconciliation screen). ND's ORIGINAL whole-library audit was written up in its log but largely never executed against the CSV -- ND was shipped 'complete' with 18 displayable LEASE_CLAUSE rows against 61-75 for CO/WY/KS/NE/MN. This row is generic boilerplate carrying no ND-specific statutory interaction; ND's ch. 47-16 was read in full from primary text this session and no conflict was identified. LIABILITY-LANGUAGE CHECK: North Dakota has NO enumerated prohibited-lease-provisions statute (confirmed this session -- § 47-16-02 is a maximum-term cap, and § 47-16-13.3 supplies only case-by-case unconscionability). The exculpation exposure that forced fixes to this clause family in KS and NE therefore does not arise for ND, same reasoning as the WY addendum. 5a.1 JUDGMENT: UNIFORM -- states field changed, clause TEXT unchanged, no re-review owed on existing tags. | OH: (1) indemnity limb — §5321.13(D) bars a tenant agreement to indemnify the landlord for liability arising under law; review scope. (2) pet rent/pet deposit must expressly carve out assistance animals — OAC 4112-5-07(C) bars ANY extra charge, though the tenant stays liable for damage the animal causes. | TX: Read in full 2026-09-25 against Tex. Prop. Code ch. 92 (full chapter, section-open; Justia 2025 host copy with per-section history lines). Applies as written. No pet, pet-deposit or entry statute in ch. 92; a pet deposit is a security deposit under s92.102 and follows Subch. C. |  SHARED EDIT 2026-09-28 (Taylor's decision, gap-discovery backfill): 'to the extent applicable law permits' added after 'without liability to Tenant', because a flat disclaimer of the landlord's own entry-and-removal act is void in states that bar exculpation (ND §9-08-02; compare OH §5321.13(D)). Raised by the ND backfill. Uniform under §5a.1: the words are self-limiting, so no state needs an override. | ND 2026-09-28: the LIABILITY-LANGUAGE CHECK above is withdrawn as to any clause disclaiming liability for the landlord's own violation of law. North Dakota does have enumerated bans outside its landlord-tenant title: N.D.C.C. §§9-08-02 (exculpation), 9-08-03 (penalties), 9-08-04 (liquidated damages) and 9-08-05 (restricting enforcement). See edu-prohibited-lease-terms-nd. | GA: Applies as written. A refundable pet deposit is a 'security deposit' (O.C.G.A. § 44-7-30(3)) inside the two-month cap and the escrow and return rules; a nonrefundable pet fee is not, and 'unpaid pet fees' may be retained from the deposit (O.C.G.A. § 44-7-34(a)). No Georgia entry or animal-removal statute located; 'to the extent applicable law permits' limits the removal sentence. s5a.1: states-only change, no propagation owed.",
  },
