  "default-by-tenant-ks-ne": {
    ruleTypes: ["REQUIRED"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-08-22",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-28",
    topicKey: "default-by-tenant",
    notes: "Same as generic default-by-tenant, minus the attorney-fee/prevailing-party sentence — K.S.A. 58-2547(a)(3) bans attorney-fee-shifting provisions outright. See edu-tenant-noncompliance-notice-ks for the 30-day/14-day-cure and 3-day pay-or-quit mechanics behind 'time period specified by applicable law.' | NE added (session 1): Neb. Rev. Stat. §76-1415(1)(c) bans attorney's-fee-shifting provisions outright, same flat rule as K.S.A. 58-2547(a)(3) — combined into one row per Taylor's direction rather than duplicating a near-identical KS-only row. | OH: R.C. 5321.13(C) bans attorney's-fee-shifting provisions bilaterally, and reaches 'any other agreement between a landlord and tenant' -- broader than either K.S.A. 58-2547(a)(3) or Neb. Rev. Stat. 76-1415(1)(c), both of which are lease-scoped only. Extended to this fee-sentence-free override (rather than the plain generic default-by-tenant, which Ohio does not use) for exactly that reason. | CURE CARVE-OUT ADDED 2026-09-26 (Taylor's decision, targeted fix at the FL sync; checklist instruction 33): the non-rent cure promise now ends 'except where applicable law permits Landlord to proceed without giving Tenant an opportunity to cure.' Before, promising a cure for every breach could contractually give up a state's no-cure termination grounds (the logs record such grounds for at least NV §40.2514, ND ch. 47-32, MN §504B.171, CA CCP §1161(4) and NE). UNIFORM: self-limiting, adds no landlord right where no such law exists. Propagation notes appended to every tagged state's log. | NC: Tagged instead of the shared `default-by-tenant` because of that clause's fee sentence. North Carolina lets a landlord recover only the litigation costs listed in N.C. Gen. Stat. § 42-46(i) (court filing fees, service costs, and attorneys' fees 'pursuant to a written lease' capped at 15% of the amount owed, or 15% of monthly rent for a non-rent default), declares it 'contrary to public policy' for a lease to provide for any other out-of-pocket expense or litigation cost of a summary ejectment or money-owed complaint (N.C. Gen. Stat. § 42-46(h)(3a)), and voids contrary lease provisions (N.C. Gen. Stat. § 42-46(h)(4)). The shared sentence's uncapped 'reasonable attorneys' fees and expenses' does not fit, and no statute supports the tenant's side (the reciprocal-fee statute, N.C. Gen. Stat. § 6-21.6, excludes consumer contracts). This fee-sentence-free variant plus the opt-in eviction-fees-nc covers the ground. NONPAYMENT: forfeiture without a forfeiture clause needs a demand and 10 days (N.C. Gen. Stat. § 42-3); where the lease provides forfeiture, summary ejectment lies under N.C. Gen. Stat. § 42-26(a)(2); tender of rent and costs before judgment ends a nonpayment case (N.C. Gen. Stat. § 42-33). The clause's 'time period specified by applicable law after ... written notice' adds a written notice duty, as in other states. INSTRUCTION 33: North Carolina's no-cure route is Article 7 (expedited eviction for criminal activity, N.C. Gen. Stat. §N.C. Gen. Stat. § 42-59 to 42-76, which 'appl[ies] to all residential rental agreements', N.C. Gen. Stat. § 42-59.1); the carve-out 'except where applicable law permits Landlord to proceed without giving Tenant an opportunity to cure' preserves it, and the opt-in criminal-activity-nc makes criminal activity a no-cure lease breach. Landlord written-notice duty for tenant breaches of N.C. Gen. Stat. § 42-43: N.C. Gen. Stat. § 42-43(b). s5a.1: states-only change, no propagation owed.",
  },
