  "assistance-animal-accommodation-ne": {
    ruleTypes: ["REQUIRED"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-08-31",
    lastChecked: "2026-08-31",
    topicKey: "assistance-animal-accommodation",
    notes: "Neb. Rev. Stat. §§20-131.01 to 20-131.04 (primary text read directly, supplied by Taylor 2026-08-31). NE RE-AUDIT: Nebraska has an express service-animal HOUSING statute the library had ZERO rows on — a genuine miss by the original pass, and the fourth consecutive state where a real finding sat outside the core landlord-tenant chapter (this is Ch. 20, not Ch. 76). §20-131.01: full and equal access to 'all housing accommodations offered for rent, lease, or compensation.' §20-131.04 carries TWO distinct fee prohibitions — 'shall not be required to pay extra compensation for such animal' AND 'shall not charge an additional deposit for such animal' — plus express tenant liability 'for any damage done to such premises by such animal.' The generic row stated a pet-fee ban but not the additional-deposit ban or the express damage-liability allocation; both are now in the clause text. Four-way classification: CATEGORY 1 — INDEPENDENT, ANIMAL-SPECIFIC, HOUSING-EXPRESS. Stronger than Kansas (generic KAAD duty only) and than Wyoming (whose §35-13-201(c) is expressly 'in accordance with the federal Fair Housing Act'); Nebraska's scheme contains no federal cross-reference in its operative housing sections. SCOPE LIMIT, and it is important: the state right reaches SERVICE animals only, not ESAs — see edu-esa-federal-only-ne. This clause is deliberately drafted to the HIGHER federal floor (Taylor's decision, 2026-08-31) and covers assistance animals generally, because the federal FHA reasonable-accommodation duty still reaches ESAs regardless of HUD's enforcement posture. Drafting to the narrower state definition would understate the landlord's actual obligation.",
  },
  "late-fee-ne": {
    ruleTypes: ["CONSTRAINED"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-08-31",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-28",
    topicKey: "late-fee",
    notes: "Neb. Rev. Stat. §76-1433, read verbatim from nebraskalegislature.gov 2026-08-31: 'Acceptance of rent with knowledge of a default by tenant or acceptance of performance by the tenant that varies from the terms of the rental agreement or rules or regulations subsequently adopted by the landlord constitutes a waiver of his right to TERMINATE THE RENTAL AGREEMENT FOR THAT BREACH, unless otherwise agreed after the breach has occurred.' THE ORIGINAL NE PASS WAS WRONG IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. (1) It OVERSTATED the consequence — the education row told landlords not to rely on the clause 'to preserve your right to act on a late payment,' but §76-1433 waives ONLY the right to terminate for that specific breach. The late fee itself, the rent, damages claims, the right to insist on timeliness going forward, and the right to terminate for any different breach all survive untouched. (2) It UNDERSTATED the exposure — it concluded the clause is 'not prohibited, just insufficient' and extended late-fee to NE unchanged. But the generic's 'or to pursue any other remedy available under this Lease' is unqualified, so it purports to preserve exactly the termination right the statute waives. Two independent problems with that: (a) §76-1433 expressly permits an agreement 'after the breach has occurred,' and expressly specifying when such an agreement is valid implies a pre-breach lease clause is not (expressio unius); (b) it is arguable — NOT settled — that a lease provision under which the tenant forgoes the §76-1433 protection is itself prohibited by §76-1415(1)(a) (tenant 'agrees to waive or to forego rights or remedies under the Act'), which matters because §76-1415(2) makes prohibited provisions unenforceable AND gives the tenant actual damages plus attorney's fees where a landlord 'deliberately uses a rental agreement containing provisions known by him or her to be prohibited.' JUDGMENT CALL SURFACED FOR TAYLOR: whether §76-1433's protection counts as a tenant 'right or remedy under the Act' is genuinely uncertain — it reads as a default rule of construction constraining the landlord rather than as a conferred tenant right. The (a) argument is available, not airtight. This override does not depend on resolving it: narrowing the sentence removes the overbreadth either way, at no cost to the landlord, since everything §76-1433 leaves alone is still preserved. | AL: Tagged instead of the base `late-fee` (same reason Nebraska took it: an identical URLTA waiver section). Alabama's acceptance-waiver rule (Ala. Code § 35-9A-424) makes acceptance of rent with knowledge of a default a waiver of the right to terminate for THAT breach unless otherwise agreed after the breach, so the base's 'or to pursue any other remedy' overstates what a standing lease sentence can preserve; this text preserves only future timely payment, later late fees and remedies for a different or continuing breach, which is lawful as written. No statutory late-fee cap or grace period (confirmed absent code-wide, `edu-no-late-fee-cap-al`). Late fees may be demanded in the 7-business-day nonpayment notice (Ala. Code § 35-9A-421(b)) and are 'rent' (all payments to the landlord under the rental agreement, Ala. Code § 35-9A-141(12)). A reservation of rights made after a breach is the only way to keep the termination right when accepting late rent (legal-tracker reservation-of-rights item, overview). Penalty doctrine for fee amounts is case law, not read (instruction 16). s5a.1: states-only change, no propagation owed.",
  },
