    notes: "NJ: Primary text read section-open 2026-09-26 from the official N.J.S.A. text supplied by Taylor, with chapter-law history lines. Supersedes default-by-tenant (base text kept verbatim; one sentence added). Controlling text: N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.66 (L.2013, c.206, s.1): if a residential lease lets the landlord recover attorney's fees or expenses (including as additional rent), the court reads in a parallel implied covenant for a tenant who successfully defends or successfully sues; tenant chooses money damages or rent credit; waiver void; a tenant who merely pays rent due after filing, with no other meritorious defense, is not 'successful'; 'expenses' = litigation costs, not travel, missed work or child care. N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.67 (L.2013, c.206, s.2): the lease clause 'shall also contain' the quoted provision in bold, at least one point larger than the rest of the clause or 11 point, whichever is larger - body reproduces it VERBATIM (capitals as enacted). Statute states no penalty for omission, but the implied covenant applies regardless. The base's own 'prevailing party' sentence is already mutual; the statutory sentence is still mandatory. Fee recovery in summary dispossess as 'additional rent' requires express lease language (Community Realty Mgmt. v. Harris, 155 N.J. 212 (1998), research pass) - this clause does not label fees 'additional rent' pending Taylor's decision; never recoverable as rent against Section 8/public-housing tenants. Other NJ notes carried from the tag screen: removal only on Anti-Eviction good cause (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1) with 2A:18-61.2 notices; mitigation required (Sommer v. Kridel, 74 N.J. 446 (1977), landlord's burden - research pass). LAYOUT: bold + point-size rule (instruction 28). Rule type REQUIRED because the base is. | NJ: DECIDED 2026-09-26 (Taylor): fees not labeled 'additional rent' (see late-fee-nj). | CURE CARVE-OUT ADDED 2026-09-26 (Taylor's decision, targeted fix at the FL sync; checklist instruction 33): the non-rent cure promise now ends 'except where applicable law permits Landlord to proceed without giving Tenant an opportunity to cure.' Before, promising a cure for every breach could contractually give up a state's no-cure termination grounds (the logs record such grounds for at least NV §40.2514, ND ch. 47-32, MN §504B.171, CA CCP §1161(4) and NE). UNIFORM: self-limiting, adds no landlord right where no such law exists. Propagation notes appended to every tagged state's log.",
