  "landlord-address-disclosure-fl": {
    ruleTypes: ["REQUIRED"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-09-26",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-26",
    topicKey: "owner-identity-disclosure",
    notes: "FL: Primary text read section-open 2026-09-26 from the official 2026 Florida Statutes (flsenate.gov / leg.state.fl.us), with per-section history lines. Controlling text: Fla. Stat. §83.50 (s. 3, ch. 2025-16 latest): in addition to any other required disclosure, the landlord or a person authorized to enter into the rental agreement for the landlord must disclose in writing, at or before commencement of the tenancy, the name and address of the landlord or a person authorized to receive notices and demands. The authorized person keeps authority until the tenant is notified otherwise; notices of names and addresses go to the residence or a tenant-specified address, or by §83.505 e-mail. Why REQUIRED here: the lease is the natural at-or-before-commencement writing. No sanction stated in §83.50; §83.55 damages; and §83.60(1)(b) lets a tenant's 7-day notice go to the §83.50 designee. No Florida registration or disclosure-as-eviction-precondition rule in Part II (contrast NJ 46:8-33).",
  },
  "pet-policy-fl": {
    ruleTypes: ["RECOMMENDED"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-09-26",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-26",
    topicKey: "pet-policy",
    notes: "FL: Primary text read section-open 2026-09-26 from the official 2026 Florida Statutes (flsenate.gov / leg.state.fl.us), with per-section history lines. Supersedes pet-policy (base not tagged FL). Why an override: the base lets the landlord 'enter the property and remove a pet, without liability to Tenant' in several situations. Pet removal is not an entry purpose in Fla. Stat. §83.53 beyond emergency or protection and preservation, removing tenant property outside surrender, abandonment or lawful eviction is barred by §83.67(5), and 'without liability' purports to limit landlord liability arising under law (void under §83.47(1)(b); actual damages for inclusion under §83.47(2)). A pet deposit is 'deposit money' (§83.43(4)) and so falls under §83.49 holding, notice and return rules. 'Pet rent' is labeled rent, so it is 'rent' under §83.43(12) (designated as rent in writing); that differs from late fees, which the library does not label rent. The indemnity sentence is not written to cover Landlord's own negligence. No Florida pet-specific landlord statute located in Part II. Assistance animals: instruction 24 family, see the FL log.",
  },
