    topicKey: "late-fee",
    notes: "AZ: Read section-open 2026-09-26/27 in the browser from azleg.gov (official legislative compilation, stated as updated through the 57th Legislature, 2nd Regular Session, 2026). azleg ARS pages print no history lines, so currency rests on the compilation date and the session-law check in the AZ log (s1.1). Why the base fails: late-fee says acceptance of a late payment does not waive Landlord's right 'to pursue any other remedy'. A.R.S. §33-1371(C) makes acceptance of rent with knowledge of a default a waiver of the right to terminate for that breach; §33-1371(A) preserves the landlord's rights on a PARTIAL payment only if the tenant agrees in a contemporaneous writing with a date the balance is due. A standing lease sentence cannot supply that writing, and purporting to waive the statutory waiver rule is a waiver of the tenant's rights (§33-1315(A)(1)). Same shape as NE §76-1433 (Addendum M.8). Controlling text: A.R.S. §33-1368(B) (reinstatement before filing requires past-due rent plus 'a reasonable late fee set forth in a written rental agreement'; after filing also fees and costs); §33-1377(F) (judgment includes 'late charges stated in the rental agreement'); §33-1371(A)-(C) (partial payments; housing assistance payments are not partial payments). NO LATE-FEE CAP in ch. 10 (full read): the only limit is that the fee be reasonable and stated in the written lease (edu-no-fee-caps-az). 'Is not Rent' follows the library default (NJ decision). OMISSION SANCTION (instruction 28): a late fee not in a written lease cannot be required for reinstatement or recovered as a lease late charge in the special detainer judgment.",
