  "eviction-fees-nc": {
    ruleTypes: ["CONDITIONAL"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-09-28",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-28",
    topicKey: "fee-shifting",
    notes: "NC: Read section-open 2026-09-28 in the built-in browser from the official General Statutes on ncleg.gov (Chapter 42 read whole on the chapter page, which prints each section's history line; the latest amendments in those history lines are S.L. 2025-45, 2025-52 and 2025-54; all 61 session laws of 2026 (S.L. 2026-1 to 2026-61) screened for Chapter 42 amendments, none found). OPT-IN LANDLORD RIGHT (instruction 30): the three administrative fees and the attorneys' fees are available only 'pursuant to a written lease', so a lease without this clause forfeits them. Controlling text: N.C. Gen. Stat. § 42-46(e) (complaint-filing fee), (f) (court-appearance fee), (g) (second trial fee), (h)(1)-(5) (one fee only; not deductible from later rent or a default ground; other administrative fees and other out-of-pocket or litigation costs 'contrary to public policy'; contrary provisions void; subsidized share), (i)(1)-(4), (j) (includable in the cure amount), (k). CURRENT TEXT OF (i)(3)-(4): taken from the session laws, not the compilation. S.L. 2024-47, s. 8 (effective 2024-09-09) added 'If the landlord is the prevailing party' to (i)(3) and a frivolous-appeal sentence; S.L. 2025-52, s. 3 and S.L. 2025-54, s. 12.4 (both approved 2025-07-02, each 'effective retroactively to September 9, 2024') struck 'If the landlord is the prevailing party' from (i)(3) and moved the frivolous-appeal rule to a new (i)(4) conditioned on the landlord prevailing. The official compilation merges the two acts badly: it prints '(4) court and the landlord is the prevailing party, delay.' followed by a '(5)' carrying the older sentence (read 2026-09-28; flagged in NC log §1.1 and instruction 46). The clause follows the enacted text. Reciprocity: none required; the reciprocal-fee statute N.C. Gen. Stat. § 6-21.6 covers business contracts only. Instruction 16: N.C. Gen. Stat. § 6-21.2 (fees on 'evidence of indebtedness', 15% cap, 5-day notice) may reach leases only through case law, not relied on. Taylor decision 2026-09-28 (NC log §6, decision 3): confirmed, and this clause should be on every NC lease by default, since leaving it off forfeits fees North Carolina allows only 'pursuant to a written lease'. The app has no library-wide default flag; 'include by default' is a per-landlord setting in the Clause Library.",
  },
