  "smoke-detectors-sc": {
    ruleTypes: ["RECOMMENDED"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-09-28",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-28",
    topicKey: "alarm-duties",
    notes: "SC: Read section-open 2026-09-28 in the built-in browser from the official South Carolina Code of Laws on scstatehouse.gov (Title 27, Chapter 40 read whole on its chapter page with every history line; the site states the Code is current through the 2025 session; the full text of every 2025 act (Act Nos. 1 to 94) and every 2026 act (Act Nos. 95 to 274) was screened for Chapter 40, Chapter 37 and landlord-tenant terms; SC log §1). Outside Title 27 (instruction 7). Controlling text: S.C. Code Ann. § 5-25-1310 (one- and two-family dwellings, including manufactured housing, must have approved, functioning smoke detectors installed to NFPA 72E (1990) and NFPA 74 (1989); 'the various requirements of this article apply only to dwellings and housing which are rental dwellings and housing'); S.C. Code Ann. § 5-25-1320 (battery units allowed in existing dwellings not remodeled over 50% of assessed value; hard-wired required for new construction after 1994-12-31 under S.C. Code Ann. § 5-25-1310(B)); S.C. Code Ann. § 5-25-1330 (owner supplies and installs; written or verbal testing and battery instructions when the tenant takes possession; tenant notifies in writing of deficiencies, received at the place of business or place held out for rent; owner repairs or replaces within fifteen days; no duty where tampered with, damaged or destroyed by the tenant and the owner notifies in writing; may assess actual cost; no duty to provide batteries); S.C. Code Ann. § 5-25-1360 (fire marshal enforcement); S.C. Code Ann. § 5-25-1370 (fifteen days to cure, then misdemeanor, $50-$200) (all 1994 Act No. 524). Apartment houses with no fire protection system: a listed detector in each dwelling unit, placement rules, and a landlord liability shield where the tenant caused the malfunction or the device was negligently produced (S.C. Code Ann. § 23-9-155; regulations by the State Fire Marshal, not read, instruction 16). Frozen-date NFPA incorporation (compare ref 294.1). No carbon monoxide alarm duty (confirmed absent, `edu-no-co-alarm-duty-sc`).",
  },
