    topicKey: "assistance-animal-accommodation",
    notes: "TN: TN-SCOPE: STATEWIDE - the same text is enacted twice, Tenn. Code Ann. § 66-28-406 for Act counties and § 66-7-111 for every county. Instruction 24 REQUIRED family: TN takes its own override of the KS-tagged base. Read section-open 2026-09-28 in the built-in browser from Justia's host copy of the 2025 Tennessee Code (the official code is published by LexisNexis), with each section's history line; currency checked against the full text of every public chapter of the 114th General Assembly (2025 and 2026 Pub. Ch. 1 to 1142 and the seven 2025 First Extraordinary Session acts, publications.tnsosfiles.com) and against the LexisNexis Tennessee Code Unannotated free public access site, which already prints the 2026 Pub. Ch. 606 sections (TN log §1). Tenn. Code Ann. §§ 66-28-406 and 66-7-111 (Acts 2019, ch. 236; 2024, ch. 754): (a) definitions - 'service animal' a dog or miniature horse individually trained; 'support animal' prescribed or recommended by a health care provider, including emotional support; 'reliable documentation' excludes website certificates sold for a fee; (b) exception request to no-pet, limit or payment policies; (c) documentation only where disability or need is not apparent; (d) verification, no confidential medical records; (e) denial if documentation not provided; (f) misrepresentation or false documentation is 'material noncompliance and default', with termination and damages including attorney's fees (repeated at Tenn. Code Ann. § 66-28-505(f)); (g) landlord NOT LIABLE for injuries by an animal permitted as an accommodation; (h) public housing exception. Tenn. Code Ann. § 66-28-204(c) (Acts 2018, ch. 960): a lease provision authorizing the landlord to hold a tenant in breach under § 66-28-505(f) 'is not unconscionable and is fully enforceable' - so the misrepresentation sentence is backed by statute (opt-in, instruction 30). Tenn. Code Ann. § 66-7-104(d) (totally or partially blind person with a guide dog: full and equal access, no extra compensation, liable for damage) and § 66-7-106(b) (no deposit for a legally blind person's guide dog; refusal a Class C misdemeanor). Direct-threat sentence rests on the federal Fair Housing Act reasonable-accommodation standard (42 U.S.C. § 3604(f); FEDERAL, instruction 16); Tennessee's statute adds no direct-threat limit of its own, and the base row's HUD-guidance caveats carry over. Criminal misrepresentation: Tenn. Code Ann. § 39-16-304 (edu-service-animal-law-tn). COUNTY-LAYER PILOT (Taylor, 2026-09-28): Tennessee is the test case for the future county/local layer. The URLTA (Tenn. Code Ann. Title 66, Chapter 28) applies only in the 17 counties over 75,000 people in the 2010 census, a list frozen by 2021 Pub. Ch. 182 § 2 (Tenn. Code Ann. § 66-28-102(a)): Anderson, Blount, Bradley, Davidson, Hamilton, Knox, Madison, Maury, Montgomery, Rutherford, Sevier, Shelby, Sullivan, Sumner, Washington, Williamson and Wilson.",
