    topicKey: "dv-lease-termination",
    notes: "TN: TN-SCOPE: STATEWIDE - identical text at Tenn. Code Ann. § 66-28-205 (Act counties) and § 66-7-112 (all counties), both Acts 2021, ch. 293. Read section-open 2026-09-28 in the built-in browser from Justia's host copy of the 2025 Tennessee Code (the official code is published by LexisNexis), with each section's history line; currency checked against the full text of every public chapter of the 114th General Assembly (2025 and 2026 Pub. Ch. 1 to 1142 and the seven 2025 First Extraordinary Session acts, publications.tnsosfiles.com) and against the LexisNexis Tennessee Code Unannotated free public access site, which already prints the 2026 Pub. Ch. 606 sections (TN log §1). (b)(1) written notice, mutually agreed release date within 30 days, and an order of protection after a hearing (§ 36-3-605) or criminal-charge documentation based on a police report; (b)(2) dated within 60 days; (b)(3) confidentiality of identifying information; (b)(4) vacate within 30 days or as agreed; (c) rent for the full month of termination plus outstanding obligations; (d)(1) co-parties not released; (d)(2) no eviction solely for victim status; (d)(3) not waivable or modifiable except the vacate date. Only leases entered into or renewed on or after 2021-07-01. L.5 DEPENDENCY READ: Tenn. Code Ann. § 36-3-601 (history to 2025, ch. 462) - 'domestic abuse victim' is anyone in the listed relationships (current or former spouses, persons who live or lived together, dating or sexual relationships, blood or adoptive relatives, relatives by marriage, and their children); 'sexual assault victim' and 'stalking victim' apply 'regardless of the relationship with the perpetrator', so a stranger stalker qualifies (contrast ND's household-member limit, L.5). Related statewide rules on evicting only the perpetrator: edu-dv-tenancy-protections-tn. COUNTY-LAYER PILOT (Taylor, 2026-09-28): Tennessee is the test case for the future county/local layer. The URLTA (Tenn. Code Ann. Title 66, Chapter 28) applies only in the 17 counties over 75,000 people in the 2010 census, a list frozen by 2021 Pub. Ch. 182 § 2 (Tenn. Code Ann. § 66-28-102(a)): Anderson, Blount, Bradley, Davidson, Hamilton, Knox, Madison, Maury, Montgomery, Rutherford, Sevier, Shelby, Sullivan, Sumner, Washington, Williamson and Wilson.",
