    topicKey: "renters-insurance-advisory",
    notes: "VA: VA-SCOPE: STATEWIDE (REQUIRED whenever the lease does not require renter's insurance; harmless and accurate when it does). Read section-open 2026-09-28 in the built-in browser on the official Code of Virginia (law.lis.virginia.gov; Title 55.1 Chapter 12 read whole from the vacodefull page, which prints the 2026-session amendments and every delayed-effective version with its history line); 2024-2026 amending chapters read in enrolled text on lis.virginia.gov with their effective-date clauses (VA log §1). Va. Code Ann. § 55.1-1206(D), second paragraph (history ends 2021, Sp. Sess. I, c. 427): 'If the rental agreement does not require the tenant to obtain renter's insurance, the landlord shall provide a written notice to the tenant, prior to the execution of the rental agreement, stating that (i) the landlord is not responsible for the tenant's personal property, (ii) the landlord's insurance coverage does not cover the tenant's personal property, and (iii) if the tenant wishes to protect his personal property, he should obtain renter's insurance'; the notice 'shall inform the tenant' that renter's insurance does not cover flood damage and advise contacting FEMA or visiting the NFIP or DCR Flood Risk Information System websites; failure does not affect validity; translation assistance or referral, no fee. The clause's first sentence is statutory content, not a lease exculpation: Va. Code Ann. § 55.1-1208(A)(5) bars the tenant agreeing to exculpate the landlord, which this notice does not ask; the shared tenants-property-insurance row (whose 'Landlord is not liable' sentence is an agreed exculpation) is not tagged, and tenants-property-insurance-ks-oh-ca is. LAYOUT/TIMING (M.12): given before execution. This is Virginia's only flood-related lease notice (edu-no-flood-disclosure-va).",
