    notes: "VA: VA-SCOPE: STATEWIDE. Read section-open 2026-09-28 in the built-in browser on the official Code of Virginia (law.lis.virginia.gov; Title 55.1 Chapter 12 read whole from the vacodefull page, which prints the 2026-session amendments and every delayed-effective version with its history line); 2024-2026 amending chapters read in enrolled text on lis.virginia.gov with their effective-date clauses (VA log §1). Va. Code Ann. § 55.1-1229 (history ends 2024, c. 46): (A)(1) consent not unreasonably withheld for the listed purposes; (A)(3) 'If the rental agreement so provides and if a tenant without reasonable justification declines to permit the landlord or managing agent to exhibit the dwelling unit for sale or lease, the landlord may recover damages, costs, and reasonable attorney fees' (OPT-IN landlord right, instruction 30, carried here; 'reasonable justification' includes a written health concern during a declared communicable-disease emergency, with a video tour instead); (A)(4) emergency entry without consent; notice of intent and reasonable times except emergency or impracticability; at least 72 hours' notice of routine maintenance not requested by the tenant, performed within 14 days, the notice stating the last possible date; no notice for tenant-requested maintenance; pandemic-emergency provisions (not repeated in the clause); (A)(5) court access order during an unlawful detainer; (B) 30-day temporary relocation for nonemergency conditions; (C) no other access except court order, §§ 55.1-1248 and 55.1-1249, abandonment or surrender; (D) tenant security devices. Va. Code Ann. § 55.1-1249 (entry during absences over seven days). Va. Code Ann. § 55.1-1223 (48-hour written pesticide notice unless the tenant requested or agrees; tenant concerns 24 hours ahead; tenant preparation per written instructions; conspicuous signs 48 hours ahead in other areas - landlord duty, not lease text). Va. Code Ann. § 55.1-1210 (injunction, termination, damages and fees either way for refused or abusive access). NOT TAGGED on the shared landlords-access row: its 24-hour notice is short of the 72-hour routine-maintenance rule for unrequested work only through its 'or longer' fallback, it limits entry to business hours and to repairs and showings, and it omits the opt-in showing-refusal remedy and pesticide notice; override written instead. Form 200 (Rev. 07/23) § 9(b) still says 24 hours for pesticide notice against the statute's 48 (VA log §15).",
