  "holdover-rate-va": {
    ruleTypes: ["CONDITIONAL"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-09-28",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-28",
    topicKey: "holdover-rate",
    notes: "VA: VA-SCOPE: STATEWIDE. OPT-IN landlord right (instruction 30); same library pattern as holdover-rate-ga/-nc/-sc/-tn, but Virginia's has a statutory basis and cap, so the SC text was not copied. Read section-open 2026-09-28 in the built-in browser on the official Code of Virginia (law.lis.virginia.gov; Title 55.1 Chapter 12 read whole from the vacodefull page, which prints the 2026-session amendments and every delayed-effective version with its history line); 2024-2026 amending chapters read in enrolled text on lis.virginia.gov with their effective-date clauses (VA log §1). Va. Code Ann. § 55.1-1253(C) (history ends 2023, c. 679): holdover without consent - action for possession plus actual damages, reasonable attorney fees and court costs 'unless the tenant proves by a preponderance of the evidence that the failure of the tenant to vacate the dwelling unit as of the termination date was reasonable'; 'The landlord may include in the rental agreement a reasonable liquidated damage penalty, not to exceed an amount equal to 150 percent of the per diem of the monthly rent, for each day the tenant remains in the dwelling unit after the termination date specified in the landlord's notice'; HUD-regulated units: not over the per diem of the monthly rent. The clause keeps the statute's 'termination date specified in the landlord's notice' trigger; where the lease simply expires, the parenthetical relies on the lease end date as the termination date, which the statute does not expressly address (noted, VA log §7). Tag companion: holdover-ca (actual damages). K.3 checked: the shared holdover row's 'maximum amount permitted by applicable law' is not tagged, because Virginia's figure exists only if the lease includes it. Form 200 (Rev. 07/23) § 28 offers 150% (100% HUD) per diem liquidated damages as an alternative to actual damages.",
  },
  "redemption-rights-va": {
    ruleTypes: ["RECOMMENDED"],
    contentType: "LEASE_CLAUSE",
    verificationStatus: "VERIFIED",
    effectiveFrom: "2026-09-28",
    lastChecked: "2026-09-28",
    topicKey: "redemption",
    notes: "VA: VA-SCOPE: MORE THAN 4 VIRGINIA UNITS (pair default; accurate for every landlord that does not limit redemption). Pair with redemption-limit-va-small, choice group va-size-redemption. Read section-open 2026-09-28 in the built-in browser on the official Code of Virginia (law.lis.virginia.gov; Title 55.1 Chapter 12 read whole from the vacodefull page, which prints the 2026-session amendments and every delayed-effective version with its history line); 2024-2026 amending chapters read in enrolled text on lis.virginia.gov with their effective-date clauses (VA log §1). Outside-title sections read section-open 2026-09-28 from the official Code of Virginia Law Library compilation (law.lis.virginia.gov/law-library, per-title CSV) overlaid with every section on the official 2026 Updates pages (VA log §17). Va. Code Ann. § 55.1-1250 (history ends 2021, Sp. Sess. I, c. 410): (A) acceptance of full payment defeats eviction for nonpayment; partial payment may be accepted with reservation only by written notice with the statutory language (edu-acceptance-of-rent-with-reservation-va); 'a landlord with four or fewer rental dwelling units, or up to a 10 percent interest in four or fewer rental dwelling units, may limit a tenant's use of the right of redemption to once per lease period, provided that the landlord provides written notice of such limitation to the tenant'; (B) redemption tender by a local government or nonprofit, 10-day continuance; (C) payment of all amounts as of the court date dismisses the unlawful detainer unless other grounds; (D) payment at least 48 hours before the scheduled eviction cancels it; the landlord must promptly notify the sheriff, and a willful failure may be treated as a § 55.1-1243.1 violation; (E) written statement of amounts on request; cashier's check, certified check or money order. Form 200 (Rev. 07/23) § 21 states the small-landlord limit as 'no more than one time during any 12-month period of continuous residency', which is not the current statutory wording ('once per lease period') - VA log §15. SIZE-TIER PILOT (Taylor, 2026-09-28, VA decision 1): Virginia's Act covers every residential landlord (the old two-single-family-home opt-out was dropped in the 2019 recodification), but several rules turn on how many Virginia rental units the landlord owns: 'more than four rental dwelling units or more than a 10 percent interest in more than four rental dwelling units, whether individually or through a business entity, in the Commonwealth'. Four pairs (choice groups va-size-*) carry the lease wording that differs; the more-than-four variant is the default because it is lawful for every landlord. Product flag: a per-state 'Virginia rental units owned' landlord attribute (VA log §14).",
  },
