    notes: "AL: Read section-open 2026-09-28 in the built-in browser from the official Code of Alabama 1975 on ALISON (alison.legislature.state.al.us, the Legislature's own code service; Title 35 Chapter 9A read whole, all 48 section numbers, with every history line; newest Chapter 9A amendment Act 2018-473; whole Code loaded, 49,638 section versions including versions effective 2026-10-01 and later; every 2024, 2025 and 2026 act touching landlord-tenant terms screened; AL log §1). OPT-IN contract right offered on Taylor's decision 2 (2026-09-28); text identical to `holdover-rate-sc` / `holdover-rate-ga`. Alabama's statutory remedy for a willful, bad-faith holdover (the greater of three months' periodic rent or actual damages, plus reasonable attorney's fees, Ala. Code § 35-9A-441(c)) is separate and not displaced by this clause; 'in place of the actual damages' refers to the contractual measure in `holdover-ca`. No Alabama statute caps or bars a stipulated holdover charge (contrast VA § 55.1-1253(C)); enforceability as liquidated damages is case law, not read (instruction 16). Acceptance of rent with knowledge of a default waives termination for that breach (Ala. Code § 35-9A-424), hence the sentence that a holdover charge is not rent for a continued tenancy.",
