    topicKey: "plain-language-consumer-statement",
    notes: "PA: Read section-open 2026-09-29 in the built-in browser on the General Assembly's official site (palegis.us) and, for court rules and regulations, the official Pennsylvania Code site (pacodeandbulletin.gov): The Landlord and Tenant Act of 1951 (1951 P.L. 69, No. 20) read whole from the official unconsolidated-statute text with every amendment note (newest: Act 88 of 2024, 2024 P.L. 944); the Pennsylvania Constitution and all 74 titles of the Pennsylvania Consolidated Statutes (75 files) loaded from the official title files (29.28 million characters; revision stamps 2022-03-07 to 2026-09-16) and searched whole; all 262 acts of 2024, 2025 and 2026 screened in full text for landlord-tenant terms; no special-session act in 2024-2026 (PA log §1). P.S. numbers are Purdon's, which the official site does not print; each 1951-Act cite gives the Act section in parentheses (kickoff rule). Controlling text: Plain Language Consumer Contract Act, 73 P.S. § 2205(d)(1) (Act § 5(d)(1), 1993 P.L. 128, No. 29, as amended 2006 P.L. 1563, No. 176): 'A contract shall have a statement that contains the following: (i) A general description of the property that may be taken or affected by reason of a security interest or contract if the consumer does not meet the terms of the contract ... (ii) Contract waivers of a consumer's rights in residential leases.' The Act covers residential leases: a 'consumer contract' includes a written agreement in which a consumer 'leases or rents ... real property' (73 P.S. § 2203); commercial leases and contracts of more than $50,000 are excluded (73 P.S. § 2204(b)(2), (8)). The Attorney General's statement of policy lists 'Does not contain contract waivers of a consumer's rights in residential leases' as a test-of-readability failure (37 Pa. Code § 307 Appendix B item 14; administrative statement of policy, instruction 16). Remedies for failing the test of readability: actual loss, $100 statutory damages, costs and reasonable attorney fees (73 P.S. § 2207(a)); UTPCPL violation (73 P.S. § 2207(b)); no liability after good-faith effort, and no class actions (73 P.S. § 2208); contract not voided (73 P.S. § 2208(c)); four-year limit (73 P.S. § 2208(b)). Wage attachment: 42 Pa.C.S. § 8127(a)(3.1)-(3.2), (f) (residential-lease judgments; 10% of net wages or the poverty-line limit; not against protected abuse victims for physical damages) - 'in some cases' is deliberate. Property left behind: 68 P.S. § 250.505a (Act § 505.1). Distress for rent is not mentioned (the library never uses it; `edu-distress-for-rent-pa`). BUILDER GAP (Addendum M.2, conditional content): the waiver list depends on which clauses the landlord selects (at present only `notice-to-quit-waiver-pa`); until the builder fills it, the bracket must be completed by hand. The Act places the statement immediately after any Truth in Lending disclosures (73 P.S. § 2205(d)(2)), which a lease does not carry.",
