    notes: "PA: Read section-open 2026-09-29 in the built-in browser on the General Assembly's official site (palegis.us) and, for court rules and regulations, the official Pennsylvania Code site (pacodeandbulletin.gov): The Landlord and Tenant Act of 1951 (1951 P.L. 69, No. 20) read whole from the official unconsolidated-statute text with every amendment note (newest: Act 88 of 2024, 2024 P.L. 944); the Pennsylvania Constitution and all 74 titles of the Pennsylvania Consolidated Statutes (75 files) loaded from the official title files (29.28 million characters; revision stamps 2022-03-07 to 2026-09-16) and searched whole; all 262 acts of 2024, 2025 and 2026 screened in full text for landlord-tenant terms; no special-session act in 2024-2026 (PA log §1). P.S. numbers are Purdon's, which the official site does not print; each 1951-Act cite gives the Act section in parentheses (kickoff rule). OPT-IN contract clause under the standing library decision (GA session 2026-09-28: a stipulated holdover rate is offered as an opt-in state clause where no statutory measure exists); text identical to `holdover-rate-al` / `holdover-rate-sc`. Pennsylvania has no statutory holdover multiplier or cap: the 1951 Act gives judgment 'for damages, if any, for the unjust detention of the demised premises' and rent due (68 P.S. § 250.503(a)(2)-(3), Act § 503, added 1995 P.L. 261, No. 36; Pa.R.Civ.P.M.D.J. 581 suspends Act 36 of 1995 only to the extent inconsistent, and Rule 514A(2) still enters judgment for damages for unjust detention); holdover search over both corpora found no residential measure (PA log §17). The Pennsylvania REALTORS® Form RL (rev. 9/17, rel. 1/18) ¶21 pre-prints holdover damages of the monthly Rent plus a stated percentage (10% in the copy compared), prorated daily, plus eviction costs and court-awarded attorney's fees (PA log §15): a contract term, not law, and not copied. Enforceability of a stipulated figure is case law (liquidated damages vs penalty), not read (instruction 16). Acceptance of rent after a holdover or notice may waive the landlord's notice (case law, not read), hence the last-but-one sentence.",
